"""Core vocabulary. Each term: (anchor, display, home question, definition).

`home` is the question that explains the term properly. Any other question that
uses the term links here instead of assuming the reader has already read it.
"""

TERMS = [
    ("endpoint", "Endpoint", 3,
     "A single URL a server exposes, combined with the HTTP methods it accepts. It is the entry point to one resource or capability, and it is what a router maps to a handler function."),
    ("rest", "REST", 1,
     "An architectural style for networked applications, defined by six constraints rather than by a wire format. An API that ignores the constraints is not RESTful just because it returns JSON."),
    ("resource", "Resource", 1,
     "The thing a URL names - a user, an order, a collection of orders. REST models the API as nouns acted on by standard HTTP methods, rather than as remote function calls."),
    ("idempotent", "Idempotent", 63,
     "A method is idempotent when repeating the same request has the same effect as sending it once. GET, PUT, and DELETE are idempotent; POST is not, which is why retrying a failed POST can double-charge a customer."),
    ("statelessness", "Statelessness", 4,
     "The rule that each request carries everything the server needs to process it. The server keeps no per-client memory between requests, so any request can be served by any instance."),
    ("status-code", "Status code", 61,
     "The three-digit result on an HTTP response: 2xx success, 3xx redirect, 4xx the client's fault, 5xx the server's fault. Clients and proxies both branch on this, so returning 200 with an error body breaks them."),
    ("persistent-connection", "Persistent connection", 2,
     "Reuse of one TCP connection for several request/response pairs, the default since HTTP/1.1. It removes a handshake per request, which is why connection pooling matters for service-to-service calls."),
    ("tcp", "TCP", 10,
     "The transport that turns IP's best-effort packets into a reliable, ordered byte stream: it opens a connection with a handshake, numbers every byte, retransmits whatever is not acknowledged, and slows itself down when the network is congested."),
    ("udp", "UDP", 10,
     "The transport that adds almost nothing to IP - ports, a length, and a checksum, in eight bytes of header. Each datagram either arrives whole or not at all, with no connection, no retransmission, and no ordering, so recovering from loss is the application's job."),
    ("head-of-line-blocking", "Head-of-line blocking", 10,
     "One stalled item holding up everything queued behind it, even data that has already arrived. It happens at the HTTP layer when a response blocks the next request on the connection, and at the TCP layer when one lost packet blocks every stream sharing that connection."),
    ("websocket", "WebSocket", 8,
     "A protocol that upgrades an HTTP connection into a long-lived, bidirectional channel. Once open, the server can push to the client without being asked, which plain HTTP cannot do."),
    ("api-versioning", "API versioning", 6,
     "Serving more than one contract at a time so existing clients keep working while the API evolves. The version can live in the URL path or in a request header."),
    ("breaking-change", "Breaking change", 6,
     "A change that makes a previously valid client request fail or be misinterpreted: removing a field, renaming one, tightening validation, or changing a status code. Adding an optional field is not one."),
    ("presigned-url", "Presigned URL", 9,
     "A time-limited URL, signed by the backend, that lets a client upload to or download from object storage directly. It keeps large file bytes out of the application server entirely."),
    ("cookie", "Cookie", 22,
     "A small key-value pair the server asks the browser to store and send back automatically on later requests to that site. It is a transport for state, not the state itself."),
    ("session", "Session", 24,
     "Server-side state belonging to one logged-in client, keyed by a session ID. The client holds only the ID; the data - user, roles, expiry - stays on the server or in a shared store."),
    ("jwt", "JWT", 21,
     "A JSON Web Token: a signed, self-describing token carrying claims such as user ID and expiry. Anyone can read it; only the holder of the key can forge one. Signed is not encrypted."),
    ("hmac", "HMAC", 28,
     "A keyed hash: a secret is mixed into the hash of the message, so only a holder of that secret can produce or check the result. The same key does both, which is why anyone who can verify an HMAC can also forge one."),
    ("jwks", "JWKS", 28,
     "A JSON Web Key Set: the document an issuer publishes its public keys at, each with a key ID that signed tokens name in their header. Verifiers fetch the key a token names, so rotating keys needs no redeploy."),
    ("same-origin-policy", "Same-origin policy", 27,
     "The browser rule that scripts on one origin - scheme, host, and port together - cannot read responses from another origin. It is the restriction CORS selectively relaxes."),
    ("cors", "CORS", 27,
     "Cross-Origin Resource Sharing: response headers by which a server tells the browser which other origins may read its responses. It is enforced by the browser and applies to nothing else."),
    ("preflight", "Preflight request", 27,
     "An automatic `OPTIONS` request the browser sends before a cross-origin request that is not simple, asking whether the real request is allowed. The answer is cacheable via `Access-Control-Max-Age`."),
    ("csrf", "CSRF", 26,
     "Cross-Site Request Forgery: an attacker causes the victim's browser to send a state-changing request to a site where the victim is already authenticated, relying on cookies being attached automatically."),
    ("samesite", "SameSite", 26,
     "A cookie attribute that tells the browser when to attach the cookie to cross-site requests. `Lax` blocks it on cross-site POSTs, `Strict` blocks it on all cross-site navigation, `None` requires `Secure`."),
    ("xss", "XSS", 25,
     "Cross-Site Scripting: attacker-supplied script executes in another user's browser with the victim site's origin and privileges, so it can read the DOM and any token reachable from JavaScript."),
    ("csp", "Content Security Policy", 25,
     "A response header restricting which script, style, and connection sources a page may use. It is a second line of defense that limits the damage of an injection you failed to escape."),
    ("output-encoding", "Output encoding", 25,
     "Escaping data for the exact context it is rendered into - HTML body, attribute, JavaScript literal, URL. The correct escaping differs per context, which is why one global sanitize step is not enough."),
    ("sql-injection", "SQL injection", 23,
     "An attack where user input is concatenated into a query and changes its structure rather than only supplying values. The fix is to send the query and the data to the database separately."),
    ("prepared-statement", "Prepared statement", 23,
     "A query sent to the database with placeholders, followed by the values. The database parses the query before it sees the data, so input can never become part of the command."),
    ("orm", "ORM", 23,
     "Object-Relational Mapping: a library that generates SQL from objects and method calls. It uses prepared statements by default, which is why it removes most injection risk incidentally."),
    ("least-privilege", "Least privilege", 23,
     "Granting an account only the permissions its job requires. An application database user that cannot `DROP` limits what any successful injection can actually do."),
    ("acid", "ACID", 17,
     "Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability: the transactional guarantees relational databases provide. They are what let you move money between two rows without inventing or losing any."),
    ("normalization", "Normalization", 12,
     "Organizing tables so each fact is stored exactly once, removing the insert, update, and delete anomalies that duplicated data causes."),
    ("denormalization", "Denormalization", 12,
     "Deliberately duplicating data to avoid a join on a read-heavy path. It trades write complexity and the risk of divergence for read speed, and is a decision, not an accident."),
    ("partitioning", "Partitioning", 13,
     "Splitting one logical table into smaller physical pieces so a query touches only the relevant piece. Vertical splits columns; horizontal splits rows by range, hash, or list."),
    ("sharding", "Sharding", 13,
     "Horizontal partitioning across separate database servers. It is the only way to scale writes past one machine, and it makes cross-shard joins and transactions expensive or impossible."),
    ("index", "Index", 16,
     "A secondary structure, usually a B-tree, that lets the database find rows without scanning the table. Each index speeds up reads and slows down every write to that table, and consumes disk."),
    ("cache", "Cache", 19,
     "A fast store holding a copy of data that is expensive to recompute or refetch. Every cache needs an answer for how entries expire and what happens on a miss."),
    ("redis", "Redis", 14,
     "An in-memory data-structure store used as a cache, session store, rate limiter, and queue. Its speed comes from keeping data in RAM, which is also why capacity and durability need explicit thought."),
    ("horizontal-scaling", "Horizontal scaling", 31,
     "Adding more instances behind a load balancer, as opposed to vertical scaling, which makes one machine bigger. It requires the instances to be interchangeable."),
    ("load-balancer", "Load balancer", 31,
     "The component distributing incoming requests across instances. It also health-checks them, which is how a failing instance stops receiving traffic."),
    ("cdn", "CDN", 31,
     "A network of edge caches serving static assets close to the user. It removes the largest and dullest slice of traffic from your origin before it ever arrives."),
    ("backpressure", "Backpressure", 31,
     "Deliberately rejecting or queueing work when the system is saturated, so it degrades instead of collapsing. Rate limits, queue bounds, and load shedding are all forms of it."),
    ("gil", "GIL", 32,
     "CPython's Global Interpreter Lock: only one thread executes Python bytecode at a time. It blocks CPU-bound parallelism in threads, but is released during I/O, which is why threads still help there."),
    ("event-loop", "Event loop", 32,
     "The single-threaded scheduler behind `asyncio`. It runs a task until the task yields with `await`, then runs another, so one blocking call stalls everything."),
    ("hash-table", "Hash table", 33,
     "The structure behind `dict` and `set`: a key is hashed to an index into an array of slots, giving average O(1) lookup. Collisions are resolved by probing, and the table resizes as it fills."),
    ("dynamic-array", "Dynamic array", 33,
     "The structure behind `list`: a contiguous block of references that is over-allocated and copied to a larger block when it fills, giving amortized O(1) append and O(n) insertion in the middle."),
    ("containerization", "Containerization", 41,
     "Packaging an application with its runtime, libraries, and configuration into an image that runs the same everywhere, isolated by kernel namespaces and cgroups rather than by a hypervisor."),
    ("unit-test", "Unit test", 51,
     "A test of one unit of behavior through its public interface, with no database, network, or filesystem. Fast and precise about where the failure is."),
    ("integration-test", "Integration test", 51,
     "A test that exercises the real endpoint over HTTP against real dependencies, proving the wiring works. Slower, and the thing that catches what unit tests mocked away."),
    ("structured-logging", "Structured logging", 52,
     "Emitting logs as key-value records rather than prose, so they can be filtered and aggregated. A request ID on every line is what makes one request traceable across services."),
    ("observability", "Observability", 52,
     "Being able to answer new questions about a running system without shipping new code, using logs, metrics, and traces together."),
    ("tls", "TLS", 29,
     "The encryption layer under HTTPS. It protects data in transit from being read or modified, and authenticates the server as the one that owns the domain. It says nothing about who the caller is."),
    ("symmetric-encryption", "Symmetric encryption", 29,
     "One shared key both encrypts and decrypts, which is fast enough for a whole connection. The hard part is that both sides must already hold the same key, and you cannot send it over the line you are trying to protect."),
    ("asymmetric-crypto", "Asymmetric cryptography", 29,
     "A key pair: the private key signs what the public key verifies, and what the public key encrypts only the private key opens. In modern TLS its job is authentication - proving the server owns the domain - while the shared secret is agreed separately by ECDHE."),
    ("forward-secrecy", "Forward secrecy", 29,
     "The property that recording traffic today and stealing the server private key tomorrow still does not decrypt it, because the session keys were derived from an ephemeral exchange rather than sent under that key. It is why TLS 1.3 removed RSA key transport."),
    ("certificate-authority", "Certificate authority", 29,
     "An organisation browsers and operating systems are shipped to trust, which signs a certificate binding a public key to a domain name. Without that signature you would have no way to tell the site's public key from an attacker's."),
    ("rate-limiting", "Rate limiting", 7,
     "Capping how many requests a caller may make in a window, to contain abuse, scraping, and accidental retry storms. Usually keyed by API key, user, or IP."),
    ("oauth2", "OAuth 2.0", 7,
     "A delegation framework that lets a third party act on a user's behalf without receiving their password. It is authorization, not authentication - that is what OpenID Connect adds."),
    ("query-plan", "Query plan", 16,
     "What the database decided to do with your query - which index, which join order, in what sequence. `EXPLAIN` prints it, and `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` adds the timings and the row counts it actually saw."),
    ("deadlock", "Deadlock", 17,
     "Two transactions each holding a lock the other needs, so neither can move. The database detects the cycle and aborts one of them, which reaches your application as an error on a statement that was correct."),
    ("n-plus-one", "N+1 query", 18,
     "One query to fetch a list, then one more per item in it. The application code shows no query at all, because the ORM issues it lazily behind an attribute access."),
    ("cache-aside", "Cache-aside", 19,
     "The usual caching pattern: check the cache, and on a miss read the source, write the value back with an expiry, and return it. The cache never talks to the database itself, so losing it costs speed rather than correctness."),
    ("cache-stampede", "Cache stampede", 19,
     "A popular key expires and every concurrent request misses at once, so the whole load arrives at the database together. Prevented with a short lock on repopulation, or by adding random jitter to expiry times."),
    ("cursor-pagination", "Cursor pagination", 62,
     "Paging by sending a pointer to the last row seen rather than a page number, so the query seeks the index directly. Constant cost at any depth, and immune to rows shifting under the reader."),
    ("idempotency-key", "Idempotency key", 63,
     "A unique value the client generates once per logical operation and reuses across retries. The server records it with the result, so a repeat returns the original response instead of doing the work twice."),
    ("at-least-once", "At-least-once delivery", 63,
     "The guarantee you actually get over an unreliable network or from a queue: a message may arrive more than once, never fewer. Exactly-once processing is achieved in the handler, not in the transport."),
    ("message-queue", "Message queue", 35,
     "A buffer between a producer and a worker, so slow work leaves the request cycle and a traffic spike becomes a backlog that drains instead of a wall of timeouts."),
    ("dead-letter-queue", "Dead-letter queue", 35,
     "Where a job goes after it has failed its retries, so it stops blocking the queue and can be inspected instead of silently disappearing."),
    ("consumer-group", "Consumer group", 39,
     "A set of Kafka consumers that share the work of reading a topic: each partition is assigned to exactly one member, so adding members spreads the load up to the partition count. A second group reads the same messages independently, from its own offsets."),
    ("schema-migration", "Schema migration", 20,
     "A versioned, reviewed change to the database structure, applied by a tool that tracks what has already run. It executes while the previous version of the application is still serving traffic."),
    ("expand-contract", "Expand and contract", 20,
     "Making a schema change in additive steps first - add, dual-write, backfill, switch reads - and only removing the old shape in a later deploy, so old and new code are both valid at every point."),
    ("microservices", "Microservices", 36,
     "An architecture of many independently deployable services. It buys independent release and scaling, and charges for it by turning every internal call into a network call that can fail."),
    ("timeout", "Timeout", 37,
     "A deadline on an outbound call. Without one, a slow dependency does not fail - it holds your worker until the pool is empty, which is how one slow service takes down another."),
    ("connection-pool", "Connection pool", 38,
     "A fixed set of open database connections the application borrows from and returns to, instead of opening one per query. It exists as much to cap how many connections reach the database as to save the handshake, because the database, not the application, decides how many it can usefully serve."),
    ("circuit-breaker", "Circuit breaker", 37,
     "A guard that watches the failure rate of a dependency and, past a threshold, fails calls immediately without attempting them. It frees your workers and gives the failing service room to recover."),
    ("health-check", "Health check", 43,
     "The endpoint a load balancer or orchestrator polls to decide whether an instance should receive traffic. Liveness means the process is alive; readiness means it can actually serve."),
    ("blue-green", "Blue-green deploy", 43,
     "Running a complete second environment on the new version and switching all traffic at once. Cutover and rollback are both instant, at the cost of double infrastructure during the switch."),
    ("canary", "Canary deploy", 43,
     "Sending a small, increasing share of real traffic to the new version while comparing its error rate and latency against the old one, so a bad release reaches few users."),
    ("dependency-injection", "Dependency injection", 71,
     "Passing a collaborator in from outside instead of constructing it inside the unit that uses it. It is what lets a test hand the unit a fake, and it is a one-line change to a constructor, not a framework."),
    ("duck-typing", "Duck typing", 72,
     "Python accepting any object that has the methods being called, without it declaring that it implements anything. `typing.Protocol` is how you write that expectation down so a type checker can verify it, without forcing the implementation to inherit."),
    ("composition-over-inheritance", "Composition over inheritance", 73,
     "Giving an object a collaborator rather than making it a subclass. Inheritance couples you to the parent's internals and is checked once, at class definition; composition can be swapped per instance and per test."),
    ("repository-pattern", "Repository pattern", 74,
     "Putting every query for one kind of object behind a single class, so business logic asks for orders rather than writing SQL. It is the pattern that makes the data layer swappable and the business logic testable."),
    ("idor", "IDOR", 7,
     "Insecure Direct Object Reference, also called broken object level authorization: the caller is authenticated, but the handler trusts the identifier in the request and returns or edits a record belonging to someone else. First in the OWASP API Security Top 10."),
    ("mass-assignment", "Mass assignment", 7,
     "Binding a request payload straight onto a model, so a caller can set a field the API never meant to expose - a role or an ownership column. The fix is an explicit input schema, and an explicit output schema so internal fields cannot ride along either."),
]
